          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Thorns, thistles, briers, and brambles principally signify the curse and consequences of sin—fruitlessness, obstruction, judgment, and destruction—with context-specific applications to worldly cares and riches, wicked people or rulers, and things destined for burning.\",\"book_summary\":\"Thorny growth signifies cursed fruitlessness, particularly worldly care and deceptive wealth that choke the word, the bramble’s false promise of protection, and worthless growth or people ending in fire.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Cursed, obstructive, and fruitless growth associated with sin and divine judgment.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The curse-ground’s fruitless and obstructive growth, manifested in choking cares, deceptive protection, and eventual judgment by fire.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement is explicit. The book compresses several recognized contextual applications into one formulation rather than rejecting the consensus’s broader curse-and-fruitlessness category. It does not mention affliction or hostile obstacles, but omission in a short entry does not establish exclusion.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book preserves the consensus’s central curse, fruitlessness, obstruction, and judgment identification and uses applications already recognized by the consensus.\",\"rationale\":\"The book’s opening phrase directly matches the consensus core rooted in Genesis 3 and Hebrews 6. Its reference to worldly care and deceitful riches matches the widely recognized interpretation of Matthew 13:22. False protection and fire correspond to Jotham’s bramble in Judges 9, while worthless thorns ending in burning correspond to 2 Samuel 23 and Hebrews 6. The book therefore synthesizes established senses rather than replacing, narrowing, or materially broadening the consensus identification.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The phrase “offer false protection” is an interpretive characterization of the bramble’s promised shadow rather than an explicit general meaning of thorns, and the entry may blur distinct passage-specific uses into a single composite symbol. Even so, this does not alter the shared core identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
